# `com.integraledger.legal_context` — UCP capability specification

Version `2026-07-30`. Served at
`https://integraledger.com/lcp/ucp/2026-07-30/legal-context`.

Configuration schema:
[`legal-context.schema.json`](https://integraledger.com/lcp/ucp/2026-07-30/legal-context.schema.json).

This document describes a technology harness. It asserts nothing about whether any agreement is lawful,
sound or enforceable, and nothing in it is a legal judgment.

## What the capability means

A Universal Commerce Protocol party that declares `com.integraledger.legal_context` is stating what it
requires of an agreement's **Agreed Terms Record (ATR)** before it will transact.

An ATR is a terms document. Its identity is the SHA-256 hash of that document's bytes, and a settlement is
*welded* to it by carrying that hash in a field the payment rail already has — so that the record and the
payment cannot later be separated, and either one can be produced from the other by a party that holds only
public data. The Legal Context Protocol (LCP) defines the record, the trust levels referred to below, and
the verification walk. This capability is the discovery half: it lets two parties find out whether their
requirements are compatible *before* a payment is attempted.

## Reading a declaration obliges nobody

**Emitting this capability commits no counterparty, and reading one commits nobody to anything.** It is a
pre-filter and nothing more. A declaration is a statement about the emitting party's own requirements; it
places no obligation on the reader, creates no relationship between the parties, and is not an offer.

Two consequences follow, and both are deliberate:

- A party that cannot meet a declared requirement learns so at negotiation time, at the cost of one fetch,
  rather than after a payment has moved.
- A party that ignores the declaration entirely is not in violation of anything. UCP prunes capabilities
  that are not in the negotiated intersection; it does not refuse over them.

## How a counterparty pre-filters on it

The declaration is carried in a UCP profile's `capabilities` map, keyed by the capability name, with the
value an array of one entry per supported version:

```json
{
  "com.integraledger.legal_context": [
    {
      "version": "2026-07-30",
      "spec": "https://integraledger.com/lcp/ucp/2026-07-30/legal-context",
      "schema": "https://integraledger.com/lcp/ucp/2026-07-30/legal-context.schema.json",
      "config": { "minimumLevel": 2, "acceptedDisputeMethods": ["aaa-icdr"] }
    }
  ]
}
```

The `config` payload is exactly what the schema describes: `minimumLevel` is required and names the lowest
LCP §3 trust level this party will accept; `acceptedJurisdictions` and `acceptedDisputeMethods` are
optional, and each is a non-empty array of non-blank strings when present. To constrain nothing, omit the
field — an empty array read literally accepts nothing, and would refuse every counterparty on earth.

A reader pre-filters by comparing the declaration against what it can produce: whether it can supply an ATR
at or above `minimumLevel`, and — where the declaration names them — whether the terms it would present fall
within the accepted jurisdictions and name an accepted dispute method. If they are incompatible, the parties
do not transact under this capability. That is the whole of the mechanism.

This capability declares no `extends`. It states a requirement rather than augmenting a vertical, so it is a
root capability: an extension of a parent capability is removed from the negotiated set whenever its parent
is absent, which would silently delete this requirement in exactly the cases where it still applies.

UCP has no `required` flag and none is invented here. Within UCP a declaration is advisory: a capability the
counterparty does not declare is pruned from the intersection, never loudly refused.

## Authority binding

UCP requires a capability to declare a `schema` URL whose origin matches the namespace authority in the
capability's name, and requires a platform to validate that binding before fetching. The name
`com.integraledger.legal_context` reverses to the authority `com.integraledger`; the schema is served from
`integraledger.com`, whose reversed labels are `com.integraledger` — a label-aligned prefix of the name. The
binding therefore holds.

That binding establishes **provenance, not trust**: it proves only that the party publishing this capability
controls `integraledger.com`. Whether to negotiate, trust or implement the capability is always the reader's
decision.

The `spec` URL — this document — is documentation and is not authority-bound by UCP. It is served from the
same origin by choice, so that one capability has one custodian.

## Versioning and immutability

`2026-07-30` is the date **this capability was defined**. It is not the date of any UCP specification
release, and it does not track one.

**Dated paths are immutable.** The documents at this version's paths will not be edited after publication.
A change to what the capability means, to the shape of its `config`, or to what a declaration obliges, is
published at a **new dated path**, and both paths continue to be served. A reader that has fetched and
cached this document may rely on it continuing to say what it says.

## What this vocabulary deliberately does not carry

The `config` payload is closed: unknown properties are rejected rather than ignored. An unknown field here
would be a requirement placed on the reader, and complying with a requirement you cannot parse is not
possible. The cost of that strictness is accepted on purpose — a future primitive is a new version at a new
path, not a tolerated extra key.

Several of LCP §4.2's policy primitives are **absent by design and will not be added in any version**. A
primitive whose value tells a counterparty the threshold at which this party stops reviewing is an
invitation to sit just underneath it; LCP §12.7 exists because of that, and publishing such a value on a
discovery surface would hand it to every reader at once.

